  <content>
  <section><title>Reading Graphs of Quadratics</title>
<para>An object is thrown upward from a height of 5 feet with a velocity of 60 feet per second. Its height, \(h(t)\), in feet after \(t\) seconds is modeled by the function \(h(t)=5+60t-16t^2\).</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Graph of the quadratic function h(t)=5+60t−16t2  on a coordinate plane, origin  O. Horizontal axis scale 0 to 4 by 1’s, labeled “time (seconds)”. Vertical axis scale 0 to 80 by 20’s, labeled “distance above ground (feet)”. Some of the points of this function are (0 comma 5), (1 comma 49), to a maximum near (1 point 9 comma 61 point 2 5) then decreasing through (2 comma 61), (3 comma 41) and (3.8 comma 0)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/32a38b72c84f4afe7093510818605bcc7a9b6d4f"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 </emphasis> -Identify the starting height.
  5 feet </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 </emphasis> -Identify the maximum height by looking at the vertex or peak of the parabola.
  approximately 62 feet </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 </emphasis> -Identify when the object reaches its maximum by matching the maximum with its matching time from the \(x\)-axis.
  approximately 1.75 seconds </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 </emphasis> -When does the object hit the ground? 
  Look at where the object has a height of 0 feet, or the zero of the function. This happens at approximately 3.75 seconds. Notice there would be another zero on the graph of the function to the left of \(x=0\), but that does not make sense in the problem because the object was thrown from 5 feet. </para>
<title> Try It: Reading Graphs of Quadratics </title>
<para>A rock is launched into the air, and its height is represented by the graph below.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b461dcedda003c376d9dfa1281928c118887b198"/></media></figure></para>

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>What is the maximum height of the rock?</para>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Here is how to find the maximum height:</para>
  <para>Look at the peak, or vertex of the parabola.</para>
  <para>The maximum height is 25 feet after 2 seconds.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
